  {
   "name": "multi_thread_weight_loading",
   "unit": "s",
   "workload": "python3 /task/bench.py",
   "reported": {
    "base": 20,
    "patch": 14,
    "comparable": false,
    "scope": "wall-clock weight Load Time for a 62 GB QwQ-32B model read from a single NVMe SSD with page cache dropped; the win is disk-bandwidth-bound (3.1 -> 4.42 GB/s) not compute",
    "scale_short": "Author's 20->14s is for a 62 GB model streamed from a specific NVMe SSD with cold page cache; the gain depends entirely on the storage device's parallel-read bandwidth. TO MAKE 1:1: load a multi-shard safetensors model from the SAME class of NVMe with the page cache dropped, timing _get_weights_iterator with num_threads=1 vs 8; a tiny model or warm page cache collapses the gap (author saw only ~0.2s from cache).",
    "scale_note": "This is CPU/IO-measurable later but NOT GPU-dependent. The speedup is purely from parallelizing shard reads to saturate NVMe bandwidth; the author confirmed in comments that with a warm page cache the improvement shrinks to ~0.2s, so magnitude is highly storage- and model-size-dependent. Direction is genuine for cold large-model loads from fast disks."
   },
